1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:05,181
Welcome to Transfer Coach. This complete walkthrough explores all thirty-eight screens:

2
00:00:05,181 --> 00:00:09,392
planning, station guidance, and recovery when a journey goes wrong.

3
00:00:09,392 --> 00:00:11,918
All journey information is demo data.

4
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:16,961
Start by planning a trip. Origin, destination, and arrive-by time appear

5
00:00:16,961 --> 00:00:21,465
together, so the journey is organised around when you need to be there.

6
00:00:22,683 --> 00:00:27,156
Open the preference chip. Balanced, Fastest, and Step-free make the main

7
00:00:27,156 --> 00:00:29,596
trade-offs visible before the journey begins.

8
00:00:32,617 --> 00:00:37,202
The transport filter offers all transport, rail only, road only, and

9
00:00:37,202 --> 00:00:41,371
no ferries. These are prototype screens showing the intended choices.

10
00:00:44,383 --> 00:00:47,923
Find routes. Each option leads with the arrival time at Main

11
00:00:47,923 --> 00:00:51,142
Hall, followed by transfer time and the number of changes.

12
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:56,305
Select the train and bus route. The overview breaks the trip

13
00:00:56,305 --> 00:01:00,979
into walking, train, transfer, and bus stages, with one clear action to start.

14
00:01:02,183 --> 00:01:05,519
The Alerts tab shows disruptions with their impact on this journey.

15
00:01:05,519 --> 00:01:09,461
An alert is useful when it explains what changes for the person travelling.

16
00:01:12,483 --> 00:01:16,241
Start the trip. The walking screen combines a highlighted route, walking

17
00:01:16,241 --> 00:01:20,683
time, and one instruction card to keep the next action easy to scan.

18
00:01:21,883 --> 00:01:26,176
At the station, a section diagram and plain instructions help locate

19
00:01:26,176 --> 00:01:31,249
the platform. This replaces technical level codes with directions that match the surroundings.

20
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:36,356
The waiting screen separates two questions: when does the train leave,

21
00:01:36,356 --> 00:01:38,842
and when will I reach the event?

22
00:01:38,842 --> 00:01:40,972
Departure and destination arrival stay distinct.

23
00:01:42,183 --> 00:01:45,624
After boarding, the stop list marks the journey stage and the

24
00:01:45,624 --> 00:01:49,378
stop to leave at. The arrival time remains available throughout the ride.

25
00:01:50,583 --> 00:01:53,017
At Central, three numbers explain the transfer:

26
00:01:53,017 --> 00:01:56,840
the bus leaves in six minutes, the walk takes three, leaving

27
00:01:56,840 --> 00:01:58,231
three minutes to spare.

28
00:01:59,450 --> 00:02:03,198
Continue to station guidance. A section diagram connects the platform to

29
00:02:03,198 --> 00:02:07,286
the bus stand, while the instruction names the sign to look for.

30
00:02:08,517 --> 00:02:11,782
If the position seems wrong, use Fix my position.

31
00:02:11,782 --> 00:02:16,861
Matching a visible landmark offers a manual fallback when indoor location cannot be trusted.

32
00:02:18,083 --> 00:02:21,237
The route offers two ways out. Both show a walking time,

33
00:02:21,237 --> 00:02:24,678
allowing the stairs and escalator route to be compared with the lift.

34
00:02:25,883 --> 00:02:28,141
Choose Exit Two to preview the lift.

35
00:02:28,141 --> 00:02:32,011
The step-free alternative uses the same instruction pattern as the other route.

36
00:02:33,217 --> 00:02:37,017
For this demonstration, switch to the stairs and escalator route.

37
00:02:37,017 --> 00:02:41,577
One pictogram, one short instruction, and a step count guide the movement.

38
00:02:42,783 --> 00:02:45,762
After changing levels, turn right after the ticket gates.

39
00:02:45,762 --> 00:02:49,734
The bold arrow and exit sign give a concrete feature to match.

40
00:02:50,950 --> 00:02:54,306
Next, cross to Stand C. The final distance and the stand

41
00:02:54,306 --> 00:02:57,052
sign make the last part of the transfer specific.

42
00:02:58,283 --> 00:03:01,012
The confirmation says, You are at Stand C.

43
00:03:01,012 --> 00:03:05,447
It confirms the location without claiming that the transfer is complete before boarding.

44
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:10,712
Now wait for Bus Three Forty-Three. The countdown, stand, and destination

45
00:03:10,712 --> 00:03:15,144
arrival are visible together, with I missed it available as a fallback.

46
00:03:16,350 --> 00:03:19,586
Board the bus. The next-stop display names Lakeside and connects the

47
00:03:19,586 --> 00:03:21,939
ride to the final walk to Main Hall.

48
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:27,146
Confirm arrival at the hall. The success state appears at the

49
00:03:27,146 --> 00:03:31,869
destination, after the trip is complete. Next, we will test the disruption branches.

50
00:03:33,083 --> 00:03:36,659
Trigger a three-minute train delay. The original departure is struck through,

51
00:03:36,659 --> 00:03:40,560
the new time is shown, and the effect on arrival is included.

52
00:03:41,783 --> 00:03:45,468
Compare routes instead of waiting automatically. The bus and train options

53
00:03:45,468 --> 00:03:48,482
are presented side by side with their arrival times.

54
00:03:49,683 --> 00:03:53,372
Choose the train alternative. The recovery screen identifies the same platform

55
00:03:53,372 --> 00:03:55,719
and the wait for the next service.

56
00:03:58,750 --> 00:04:02,542
Now simulate a missed train. Recovery starts with two ranked next

57
00:04:02,542 --> 00:04:05,990
moves, each showing arrival time and minutes before the event.

58
00:04:07,217 --> 00:04:10,100
The other-option link keeps the later train available.

59
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:14,425
Recovery should preserve a clear choice rather than force a single recommendation.

60
00:04:17,450 --> 00:04:21,066
Choose the direct bus this time. Selecting the route starts guidance

61
00:04:21,066 --> 00:04:24,681
to Stand A immediately, avoiding a dead end after the decision.

62
00:04:25,883 --> 00:04:30,343
At Stand A, the bus number, departure countdown, and arrival time

63
00:04:30,343 --> 00:04:32,776
confirm the replacement service before boarding.

64
00:04:35,783 --> 00:04:40,174
Simulate passing Central. The recovery screen names the current location, explains

65
00:04:40,174 --> 00:04:43,368
how to return, and gives the next action.

66
00:04:44,583 --> 00:04:48,769
Continue to the return train. The platform, countdown, and direction of

67
00:04:48,769 --> 00:04:52,954
travel lead the screen, helping the traveller act after the mistake.

68
00:04:54,183 --> 00:04:58,100
The next screen confirms the ride back to Central and shows

69
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:01,661
the revised arrival time. Recovery includes reassurance about the outcome.

70
00:05:04,683 --> 00:05:09,160
Trigger an eight-minute bus delay. The new departure, original time, and

71
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:12,822
delay amount appear together. Arrival already includes the delay.

72
00:05:14,050 --> 00:05:17,486
Compare routes. Staying at Stand C and walking to Stand D

73
00:05:17,486 --> 00:05:20,609
can be judged by their waits and destination arrival times.

74
00:05:21,817 --> 00:05:25,442
First, stay at Stand C. The interface confirms the original option

75
00:05:25,442 --> 00:05:28,738
with its updated departure and the arrival it leads to.

76
00:05:31,750 --> 00:05:35,420
The dedicated Stay or switch scenario brings the same comparison into

77
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:38,090
view when a faster nearby service is available.

78
00:05:39,317 --> 00:05:42,735
Choose Stand D. The selected alternative opens a walking map immediately,

79
00:05:42,735 --> 00:05:46,153
with a time estimate and the taxi bay as a landmark.

80
00:05:47,383 --> 00:05:51,773
At the new stand, confirm Bus Four Ten, the stand letter,

81
00:05:51,773 --> 00:05:54,567
departure time, and destination arrival before boarding.

82
00:05:57,583 --> 00:05:59,851
The screen index can revisit any state.

83
00:05:59,851 --> 00:06:03,737
Return to the bus waiting screen to test the manual missed-service path.

84
00:06:04,950 --> 00:06:07,675
Select I missed it. The app offers a new choice:

85
00:06:07,675 --> 00:06:11,489
walk to Bus Four Ten or remain at the same stand for another service.

86
00:06:12,717 --> 00:06:15,574
The other-option link retains the same-stand route.

87
00:06:15,574 --> 00:06:20,064
Both recovery paths remain available and identify the resulting arrival time.

88
00:06:23,083 --> 00:06:27,306
Select the earlier-arriving bus card. The choice takes the traveller straight

89
00:06:27,306 --> 00:06:30,377
into walking guidance, using the same recovery pattern.

90
00:06:36,983 --> 00:06:39,763
Finally, test the Lost in the station scenario.

91
00:06:39,763 --> 00:06:43,584
The landmark screen is also available from this disruption control, as

92
00:06:43,584 --> 00:06:45,669
well as from the route itself.

93
00:06:48,683 --> 00:06:52,161
Show hotspots reveals the tappable regions for review.

94
00:06:52,161 --> 00:06:57,377
The prototype also provides Back, Next, Restart, and a complete screen index.

95
00:07:11,183 --> 00:07:16,269
All thirty-eight screens have now been explored, including all six disruption

96
00:07:16,269 --> 00:07:20,892
scenarios. Planning, guidance, and recovery share a consistent next-action structure.

97
00:07:22,117 --> 00:07:25,787
Restart returns to the welcome screen. Transfer Coach keeps the key

98
00:07:25,787 --> 00:07:29,457
questions visible: can I make the connection, where do I go,

99
00:07:29,457 --> 00:07:31,458
and what should I do next?
